---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="AI/ML Deep Dives">
<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 6.5rem 2rem 4rem;
    max-width: 860px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .hero-eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: .625rem;
    font-size: .72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--purple-l);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-family: var(--ff-mono);
  }
  .hero-eyebrow::before {
    content: '';
    width: 18px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--purple);
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.4rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -.03em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, var(--text) 40%, var(--muted2));
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    background-clip: text;
  }
  .hero p {
    font-size: 1.075rem;
    color: var(--muted2);
    max-width: 560px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }
  .posts-section {
    max-width: 860px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 2rem 7rem;
  }
  .section-rule {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: .875rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .section-rule h2 {
    font-size: .72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: var(--ff-mono);
  }
  .section-rule::after {
    content: '';
    flex: 1;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--border);
  }
  .post-card {
    display: block;
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color .2s, transform .2s, box-shadow .2s;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .post-card::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--purple-dim) 0%, transparent 55%);
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity .3s;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .post-card:hover {
    border-color: rgba(139,92,246,.5);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 40px rgba(139,92,246,.1);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .post-card:hover::before { opacity: 1; }
  .post-featured-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: .3rem;
    font-size: .68rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: .07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--amber);
    background: rgba(245,158,11,.09);
    border: 1px solid rgba(245,158,11,.22);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: .18rem .55rem;
    margin-bottom: .875rem;
    font-family: var(--ff-mono);
  }
  .post-card .tags { margin-bottom: .875rem; }
  .post-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: .5rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .post-card p {
    font-size: .875rem;
    color: var(--muted2);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .post-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    font-size: .775rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .post-arrow {
    margin-left: auto;
    color: var(--purple-l);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    transition: transform .2s;
  }
  .post-card:hover .post-arrow { transform: translateX(4px); }
  footer {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: .775rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-eyebrow" data-i18n="hero-eyebrow">Personal Research Blog</div>
  <h1 data-i18n="hero-title">Exploring the frontier of AI &amp; ML</h1>
  <p data-i18n="hero-desc">Deep dives into research papers, model architectures, and open-source implementations — with interactive visualizations and annotated source code.</p>
</section>

<section class="posts-section">
  <div class="section-rule"><h2 data-i18n="section-latest">Latest Posts</h2></div>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/representation-autoencoders-v2/">
    <div class="post-featured-badge" data-i18n="post-badge">★ Latest</div>
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Diffusion Models</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Image Generation</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>RAEv2: 10× Faster Image Generation with Smarter Encoder Layers</h3>
    <p>Three elegant insights — summing the last K encoder layers for a free reconstruction-generation trade-off, combining RAE with REPA (they're complementary, not redundant), and reusing the REPA head for internal guidance with no extra model or forward pass — cut diffusion training from 800 to 80 epochs while setting a new state-of-the-art gFID of 1.06.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~10 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/bitter-lesson-data-filtering/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Data Curation</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Scaling Laws</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Pretraining</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>A Bitter Lesson for Data Filtering</h3>
    <p>Filtering training data to keep only "high-quality" content is almost universally assumed to be necessary. Stanford researchers challenge this: with enough compute and large enough models, unfiltered Common Crawl outperforms every standard filter — and models even benefit from shuffled-word junk. Interactive crossover charts show exactly when and why the ranking flips.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~14 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/dynamo-llm/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">LLM Serving</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Energy Efficiency</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Systems</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>DynamoLLM: Cutting 53% of LLM Serving Energy Without Touching Accuracy</h3>
    <p>Current LLM clusters run every request — from "Hello!" to a 4,000-token code review — at TP8 max-frequency, wasting 3× energy on short requests. DynamoLLM routes requests to per-type pools with tuned tensor parallelism and GPU frequency, saving 53% energy, 38% CO₂, and 61% cost while meeting all latency SLOs. Interactive energy landscape shows how the optimal config shifts completely across request types.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~14 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/opd-failure-modes/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">LLM Training</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Distillation</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">RLVR</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>The Many Faces of On-Policy Distillation: Pitfalls, Mechanisms, and Fixes</h3>
    <p>On-policy distillation can catastrophically collapse or silently fail depending on three subtle traps: prefix mismatch (teacher accuracy drops 62%→46%), biased TopK gradients (causes training collapse), and a structural OPSD limitation where instance-specific PI averages to a weak policy. Interactive visualizations show each failure mode and the fixes that resolve them.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~14 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/fast-blt/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Byte-Level LMs</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Diffusion Generation</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Speculative Decoding</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>Fast Byte Latent Transformer: Generating Multiple Bytes at Once</h3>
    <p>BLT-D adds block-wise diffusion to byte-level language models — instead of one byte at a time, the decoder unmasks an entire block in parallel, cutting memory bandwidth by up to 92%. BLT-S and BLT-DV add speculative verification for lossless or near-lossless quality at 52–77% lower bandwidth.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~15 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/gated-deltanet-2/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Linear Attention</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Recurrent LLMs</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Delta Rule</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>Gated DeltaNet-2: Untying Erase and Write in Linear Attention</h3>
    <p>Every delta-rule recurrent model (DeltaNet, KDA) uses a single scalar to control both erasing stale memory and writing new content. Gated DeltaNet-2 (NVIDIA, 2026) splits these into independent channel-wise gates, improving multi-key retrieval accuracy by 35% over KDA at 4K context while preserving efficient chunkwise training.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~10 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/oscar-int2-kv-cache/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Quantization</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">LLM Serving</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>OSCAR: 2-bit KV Cache Without the Accuracy Cliff</h3>
    <p>How attention-aware covariance rotations make INT2 KV-cache quantization both accurate and deployable — closing the BF16 accuracy gap to 3.78 points on Qwen3 while delivering 8× memory reduction and 3–7× throughput gains in production SGLang serving.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2025</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~12 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/cola-dlm/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Diffusion Language Models</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Continuous Latent Space</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Flow Matching</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>Cola DLM: When Diffusion Stops Recovering Tokens and Starts Thinking in Latents</h3>
    <p>Cola DLM (ByteDance Seed, 2026) separates text generation into global semantic organization — a block-causal DiT running flow matching over compressed latent blocks — and local token realization via a conditional decoder. It outperforms matched 2B AR and LLaDA baselines at 2000 EFLOPs on reasoning tasks, while exposing a fundamental PPL-vs-generation mismatch in continuous latent models.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~18 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/ptrm/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Tiny Recursive Model</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Test-Time Compute</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Latent-Space Reasoning</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>A 7M-Param Model That Beats GPT-5 at Reasoning Puzzles</h3>
    <p>PTRM (Probabilistic Tiny Recursive Model) injects Gaussian noise into a 7M-parameter model's hidden state, runs K parallel guesses, and lets the model pick its own best answer — scoring 91.2% on pencil puzzles vs 55.1% for a 7-LLM ensemble, at 0.0001× the cost. A visual deep-dive into latent basins, width vs depth scaling, and the Q-head trick.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~16 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/elf-continuous-dlm/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Diffusion Language Models</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Flow Matching</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Continuous Embeddings</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>ELF: Continuous Diffusion for Language Finally Works</h3>
    <p>How Embedded Language Flows (MIT, 2026) beats leading discrete diffusion models using 10× fewer training tokens — by staying in continuous embedding space for the entire denoising trajectory and discretizing only at the final step. With animated flow visualizations and pseudocode walkthroughs.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~18 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/speculative-decoding-compare/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Speculative Decoding</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Comparison</span>
    </div>
    <h3>Speculative Decoding Methods Compared</h3>
    <p>Side-by-side comparison of six speculative decoding systems — EAGLE-3, DeepSeek MTP, DFlash, P-EAGLE, DART, and Gemma 4 MTP. Pick any two to compare: backbone access signal, draft architecture, inference cost, and core tradeoffs.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 26, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~10 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <a class="post-card" href="/posts/gemma4-mtp/">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="tag tag-purple">Gemma 4</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Multi-Token Prediction</span>
      <span class="tag tag-cyan">Speculative Decoding</span>
      <span class="tag tag-green">Interactive</span>
    </div>
    <h3>How Gemma 4's Multi-Token Prediction Works</h3>
    <p>A deep dive into the Gemma4 Assistant architecture: KV sharing between the backbone and draft model, centroid-based vocabulary prediction, and bidirectional attention masks — with animated visualizations and annotated source code.</p>
    <div class="post-meta">
      <span>May 24, 2026</span>
      <span data-i18n="post-read">~15 min read</span>
      <span class="post-arrow">→</span>
    </div>
  </a>
</section>

<footer data-i18n="blog-footer">Built with curiosity. Source on GitHub.</footer>
</BaseLayout>
